# textbook_coa_pds_algo.py
# Deep, comprehensive point-by-point ChatGPT style tutorials for all 11 chapters of COA, PDS & ALGO

CHAPTERS_COA_PDS_ALGO = {('COA', 0): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Machine Instructions & Addressing Modes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Four Ways to Find Treasure', 'text': "• Immediate: Having $50 cash right inside your wallet (operand is inside the instruction itself).\n• Direct: Looking at an address slip that says 'Go to Locker 405' (address field points straight to data in RAM).\n• Indirect: Looking at a slip that says 'Go to Desk 12 to find the key for Locker 405' (a pointer to a pointer).\n• Indexed / Base: Your room is 'Building B, Room 14' (Base Address of Building B + Room Offset 14)."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '9.1', 'title': 'Effective Address (EA) & Memory References in Addressing Modes', 'content': "• <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The actual physical memory address where the operand's data is stored in RAM.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Different addressing modes calculate EA in different ways to give programmers flexibility and speed.<br/><br/>• <b>Summary Table of Addressing Modes & Memory References:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Mode              Effective Address (EA)    Operand Location   Memory Accesses (Fetch + Execute)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Immediate         No EA (part of opcode)    Inside Instruction 1 access (Fetch only)<br/>Direct (Absolute) EA = Address Field        M[Address Field]   2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>Indirect          EA = M[Address Field]     M[M[Address]]      3 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Ptr + 1 Read)<br/>Register          No EA (CPU Register)      Register R         1 access (Fetch only - very fast!)<br/>Register Indirect EA = [Register R]         M[[Register R]]    2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>PC-Relative       EA = PC + Offset          M[PC + Offset]     Used in Jumps/Branches<br/>Indexed / Base    EA = Base Reg + Index Reg M[Base + Index]    Used in Array Traversals</div>• <b>Step-by-Step Example of Indirect Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose instruction <code>LOAD @500</code> is fetched from memory address 1000:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <i>Step 1 (Instruction Fetch):</i> CPU accesses address 1000 to read <code>LOAD @500</code> (1st memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <i>Step 2 (Pointer Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 500. Memory location 500 contains the number 800 (2nd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <i>Step 3 (Operand Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 800 to get the actual data value 42 (3rd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total memory accesses = 3.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, PC is ALWAYS incremented to point to the next instruction BEFORE adding the offset: EA = (PC + 1 or PC + 2) + Offset. Watch out for word-length vs byte-length PC increments!'}, {'number': '9.2', 'title': 'Instruction Word Formats & Expanding Opcode Technique', 'content': '• <b>Why Expanding Opcodes?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An instruction has a fixed bit width (e.g. 16 bits).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If some instructions need 3 register addresses and others need only 1, we can shorten or expand the opcode bits so no bits are wasted.<br/><br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Bit Budget Calculation:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Consider a 16-bit instruction format with 16 CPU registers (requires 4 bits per register since 2^4 = 16):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>3-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (4 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) | Reg3 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Total 4-bit opcode patterns = 2^4 = 16. If we allocate 14 patterns for 3-register instructions, <b>2 patterns remain free</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>2-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (8 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The 2 unused patterns expand across the 4 extra bits: <b>2 × 2^4 = 32 instructions possible</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Expanding Opcode Formula: If k patterns are left unused at the higher level, they multiply by 2^(extra bits freed) at the next level: New Instructions = (Unused Patterns) × 2^(Freed Bits) - Allocated.'}, {'number': '9.3', 'title': 'RISC vs CISC Architecture Differences', 'content': '• <b>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Simple, uniform 1-cycle instructions.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strict Load/Store Architecture:</b> Arithmetic operations (ADD, SUB) can ONLY operate on CPU registers, NEVER directly on RAM!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hardwired control unit (faster), large register file, heavily pipelined.<br/>• <b>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Variable length, multi-cycle complex instructions (e.g. <code>ADD [1000], [2000]</code> directly adds memory locations).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Microprogrammed control unit with fewer general-purpose registers.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In RISC, ALU instructions CANNOT access memory! To add a memory variable to a register, you must do: LOAD R1, [Memory] -> ADD R2, R2, R1.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Memory References in Addressing Modes', 'problem': 'An instruction stored at memory address 2000 has opcode and address field 500. Memory at 500 contains 900, and memory at 900 contains operand 75. How many total memory cycles are executed to fetch and run this instruction in Indirect Addressing Mode?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Fetch instruction from PC address 2000: 1 memory read cycle.', 'Step 2: Decode instruction: mode is Indirect. Read address field 500 to get pointer: 1 memory read cycle (returns 900).', 'Step 3: Read effective address 900 to get operand 75: 1 memory read cycle.', 'Step 4: Total memory references = 1 (fetch) + 1 (pointer fetch) + 1 (operand read) = 3 cycles.'], 'answer': 'Total memory references = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not forget to count the 1st memory reference needed to fetch the instruction itself!', 'Immediate addressing requires 1 memory reference for instruction fetch, but 0 references for operand fetch.']}, ('COA', 1): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'ALU, Control Unit & Memory Interfacing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Shorthand Math Trick & Direct Highway Delivery', 'text': "• Booth's Algorithm: Like calculating 99 × 6 by doing (100 - 1) × 6 = 600 - 6 = 594. Instead of adding 99 six times, you subtract once and add once!\n• DMA: Like a shipping company delivering pallets directly to the store warehouse without waiting for the CEO to personally inspect every single carton."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '10.1', 'title': "Booth's Multiplication Algorithm for Signed 2's Complement", 'content': "• <b>Why Booth's Algorithm?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Standard binary multiplication fails for negative signed numbers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Booth's algorithm works directly on 2's complement numbers and speeds up multiplication by skipping runs of consecutive 1s.<br/><br/>• <b>The Decision Table (Inspect bit pair Q_0 and Q_{-1}):</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Q_0  Q_{-1}   Meaning             Action Taken<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/> 0     0     Run of zeros        Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)<br/> 0     1     End of run of 1s    Accumulator A = A + Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     0     Start of run of 1s  Accumulator A = A - Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     1     Run of ones         Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)</div>• <b>Crucial: Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Shift all bits to the right by 1 position.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The Most Significant Bit (MSB, Sign Bit) is <b>REPLICATED</b> (kept identical to preserve the negative sign!).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Worst case for Booth's algorithm is alternating bits like 01010101 (requires n additions/subtractions). Best case is all 1s (11111111) or all 0s (only 1 addition/subtraction or 0)!"}, {'number': '10.2', 'title': 'Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Units', 'content': '• <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Built using logic gates, flip-flops, and decoders.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Pros:</b> Maximum possible speed. Used in high-performance RISC CPUs.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Cons:</b> Impossible to modify or add instructions without replacing physical chips.<br/><br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Control signals are stored as micro-instructions inside a Control Memory (ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Horizontal Microprogramming:</b> 1 bit per control signal. Fast, unencoded, but control words are very wide.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Vertical Microprogramming:</b> Signals are grouped into mutually exclusive fields. If a field has k signals, it requires <b>⌈log2(k + 1)⌉ bits</b> (the +1 is for the NO-OP inactive state!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In vertical microprogramming, always add 1 for the inactive state before taking log2: Bits = ⌈log2(Number of Signals + 1)⌉!'}, {'number': '10.3', 'title': 'DMA (Direct Memory Access) Data Transfer & Cycle Stealing', 'content': '• <b>What is DMA?</b> Allows I/O devices (hard drives, NICs) to transfer data directly to/from RAM without routing through the CPU.<br/>• <b>Cycle Stealing Mode:</b> DMA controller takes control of the memory bus for 1 bus cycle to transfer 1 data word, then releases it back to CPU.<br/>• <b>Formula for Percentage CPU Slowdown:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>% CPU Time Stolen = (Device Transfer Rate / Bus Bandwidth) × 100%</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: If device prepares 1 byte every 4 µs and bus transfer takes 0.5 µs:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;CPU cycle stolen fraction = 0.5 µs / 4 µs = 12.5%.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DMA calculations, verify the units! Bus bandwidth and device preparation speed must be converted to the same time base (e.g. bytes per second) before dividing.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage', 'problem': 'A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec in cycle stealing mode. The system memory bus has a transfer speed of 10 MB/sec. What percentage of CPU cycle time is consumed by DMA?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Device data transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.', 'Step 2: Bus transfer bandwidth = 10 MB/sec.', 'Step 3: Percentage of CPU cycles stolen = (Device Rate / Bus Rate) × 100%.', 'Step 4: Calculation: (2 MB/s / 10 MB/s) × 100% = 0.2 × 100% = 20%.'], 'answer': 'CPU cycle time consumed = 20%.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In vertical microprogramming, forgetting the +1 for the NO-OP state is the #1 student mistake in GATE!', "In Booth's algorithm, shifting is ARITHMETIC shift right, never logical shift right (do NOT shift in a 0 on the left if the number is negative!)."]}, ('COA', 2): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping & AMAT', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Desk, Bookshelf & Public Library', 'text': '• Registers: The pen and paper right in your hands (0 nanoseconds).\n• Cache: The small bookshelf right beside your desk (a few seconds).\n• Main Memory (RAM): The library across the college campus (10 minutes).\n• Secondary Storage (Disk): Ordering a book by postal mail from another state (days)!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '11.1', 'title': 'Cache Address Partitioning (Direct, Associative, Set-Associative)', 'content': "• <b>Every memory address is partitioned into 3 fields:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Direct Mapped:       [ Tag Bits        | Line Bits (Index) | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>k-Way Set-Associative:[ Tag Bits        | Set Bits (Index)  | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>Fully Associative:   [ Tag Bits        | No Index Bits     | Block/Word Offset ]</div>• <b>Formulas to Calculate Field Widths:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Word / Byte Offset Bits:</b> <code>log2(Block Size in bytes)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Number of Cache Lines:</b> <code>Total Cache Size / Block Size</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Number of Sets:</b> <code>Total Cache Lines / k</code> (for k-way set associative).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Set Bits:</b> <code>log2(Number of Sets)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Tag Bits:</b> <code>Total Physical Address Bits - Set Bits - Word Offset Bits</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tag Directory Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Tag Directory Size = (Total Cache Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Total cache capacity stores only DATA! The Tag Directory is extra hardware overhead: Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag + 1 Valid + 1 Dirty bit)!'}, {'number': '11.2', 'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Model 1: Hierarchical Access (L1 checked first; on miss, lower level is visited):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = t_{hit_L1} + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If miss penalty goes to L2 cache: Miss_Penalty_L1 = t_{hit_L2} + (Miss_Rate_L2 × t_{mem}).<br/><br/>• <b>Model 2: Simultaneous Access (CPU queries Cache and Memory in parallel):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = (Hit_Rate × t_{cache}) + (Miss_Rate × t_{mem})</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Cache Write Policies:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Through:</b> Writes to both cache and RAM simultaneously. Safe, simple, but slow; needs Write Buffer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Back:</b> Writes only to cache. RAM updated only when a modified block is evicted. Fast; requires <b>Dirty Bit</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless an exam question explicitly says 'Simultaneous Access', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes access time to the next lower level!"}, {'number': '11.3', 'title': 'The 3 Cs of Cache Misses', 'content': '• <b>1. Compulsory Miss (Cold Start):</b> First-ever access to a block. Unavoidable regardless of cache size.<br/>• <b>2. Capacity Miss:</b> Cache is physically too small to hold all active working set blocks. Occurs even in Fully Associative caches.<br/>• <b>3. Conflict Miss (Collision):</b> Multiple memory blocks map to the same set/line even though empty lines exist elsewhere.<br/>• <b>Key Insight:</b> Fully Associative cache has <b>ZERO conflict misses</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Increasing associativity (e.g. 2-way to 4-way) reduces Conflict misses, but increases hit time because the tag comparator logic becomes wider and more complex.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Full Cache Address Splitting', 'problem': 'A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find Tag bits, Set bits, Word offset bits, and Tag directory size (assume 1 valid bit).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Word offset = log2(32 bytes) = 5 bits.', 'Step 2: Total lines = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.', 'Step 3: Number of sets = 2048 lines / 4 lines per set = 512 sets.', 'Step 4: Set index bits = log2(512) = 9 bits.', 'Step 5: Tag bits = 32 - (9 + 5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.', 'Step 6: Tag directory size = 2048 lines × (18 tag bits + 1 valid bit) = 2048 × 19 bits = 38,912 bits = 4864 bytes.'], 'answer': 'Tag = 18 bits, Set = 9 bits, Offset = 5 bits, Tag Directory = 4864 bytes.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In word-addressable systems, word offset bits depend on word size (e.g. if 1 word = 4 bytes, 32-byte block holds 8 words ⇒ offset is 3 bits, not 5 bits!).', 'Direct-mapped cache is simply a 1-way set-associative cache; fully associative cache has exactly 1 set.']}, ('COA', 3): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Pipeline Hazards, Speedup & Instruction Level Parallelism', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Laundromat Assembly Line', 'text': '• Non-Pipelined: You wash load 1 (30 min), dry (30 min), fold (30 min), iron (30 min). Total = 2 hours per load. 4 loads take 8 hours!\n• Pipelined: As soon as load 1 finishes washing and enters the dryer, you drop load 2 into the washer! After a warm-up period, one complete load finishes every 30 minutes! Throughput increases 4x.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '12.1', 'title': 'The 5-Stage Classic RISC Pipeline & Clock Cycle', 'content': '• <b>The 5 Pipeline Stages:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>IF (Instruction Fetch):</b> Read instruction from Instruction Cache into IF/ID register.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>ID (Instruction Decode / Register Read):</b> Decode opcode and read source register operands.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>EX (Execute / Address Calculation):</b> ALU performs operation or computes effective memory address.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>MEM (Memory Access):</b> Read or write data from/to Data Cache (for LOAD/STORE).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>WB (Write Back):</b> Write computed result back into destination register.<br/><br/>• <b>Clock Cycle Period Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Clock Period (τ) = max(Stage Delays) + Pipeline Register Delay</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;The slowest stage sets the speed for all stages!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Pipeline clock cycle cannot be faster than the slowest stage! If stages take 100ps, 150ps, 250ps, 120ps, 110ps with 20ps buffer, Clock Period = 250 + 20 = 270ps.'}, {'number': '12.2', 'title': 'The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards & Solutions', 'content': '• <b>1. Structural Hazard (Hardware Resource Conflict):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Two instructions need the same hardware simultaneously (e.g. IF fetching instruction while MEM reads data from single-port RAM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Separate Instruction Cache & Data Cache (Harvard Architecture).<br/><br/>• <b>2. Data Hazard (RAW - Read After Write):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Instruction 2 needs register result calculated by Instruction 1 before Inst 1 writes it back in WB stage.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing)</b> feeds result directly from EX/MEM output to EX input without waiting for WB!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Instructions):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Target address not known until ID or EX stage, causing wrong instructions to be fetched.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Branch Prediction, Branch Target Buffer, Delayed Branching.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In standard in-order pipelining, only RAW (True Dependency) causes pipeline stalls! WAR and WAW hazards only occur in out-of-order processors.'}, {'number': '12.3', 'title': 'Pipeline Speedup & Effective CPI', 'content': '• <b>Execution Time:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Non-pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_nonpipe = n × k × τ</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_pipe = (k + n - 1 + Stalls) × τ</code>.<br/>• <b>Speedup (S):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Speedup = T_nonpipe / T_pipe</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For large n (n → ∞) with no stalls: <b>Maximum Ideal Speedup = k</b> (number of stages!).<br/>• <b>Effective CPI:</b> <code>CPI = 1 + (Stalls per instruction)</code>. Actual Speedup = <code>k / CPI</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If 20% of instructions are branch instructions and each branch causes 2 stall cycles: Effective CPI = 1 + (0.20 × 2) = 1.4! Speedup over non-pipelined = k / 1.4.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Pipeline Speedup Calculation', 'problem': 'A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 2 ns, 1.5 ns, 3 ns, 2 ns, 1 ns. Register delay is 0.5 ns. In non-pipelined mode, an instruction takes 10 ns. Calculate the clock period and maximum speedup for a large number of instructions without stalls.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Clock period τ = max(2, 1.5, 3, 2, 1) + 0.5 = 3 + 0.5 = 3.5 ns.', 'Step 2: Non-pipelined time per instruction = 10 ns.', 'Step 3: Pipelined time per instruction for large n (ideal CPI = 1) = 1 × 3.5 ns = 3.5 ns.', 'Step 4: Speedup S = 10 ns / 3.5 ns = 2.857.'], 'answer': 'Clock period = 3.5 ns; Speedup = 2.86x.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Speedup is k ONLY when all stage delays are perfectly identical and register delay is 0!', 'Even with operand forwarding, a LOAD instruction immediately followed by an ALU instruction that uses the loaded value still requires 1 STALL cycle (Load-Use data hazard)!']}, ('PDS', 0): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Programming in C & Recursion Tracing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Russian Matryoshka Doll (Recursion)', 'text': '• Recursion: Opening a nesting doll to find a smaller doll inside. You keep unpacking until you hit the smallest solid doll (Base Case). Then you close them all back together (Call Stack unwinding)!\n• Array Decay: Handing someone a business card with your address written on it instead of physically moving your whole house to them.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '13.1', 'title': 'Pointers & Array Equivalence in C (Deep Tutorial)', 'content': "• <b>1. Visualizing Memory: How an Array is Stored in C:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose you write: <code>int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};</code> (assuming 4 bytes per integer):<br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Index:      arr[0]      arr[1]      arr[2]      arr[3]<br/>Value:        10          20          30          40<br/>Address:    [1000]      [1004]      [1008]      [1012]<br/>               ↑<br/>            arr (points here)</div>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The array name <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer:</b> It points to the first element: <code>arr == &arr[0] == 1000</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Because <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer, you <b>cannot reassign it</b> (<code>arr++</code> causes a compilation error!).<br/><br/>• <b>2. The Subscript Rule: Why does <code>3[arr] == arr[3]</code> work?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• In C, square brackets <code>[]</code> do not exist in machine code! C compiler translates <code>arr[i]</code> into:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i)</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pointer addition automatically scales by type size: <code>Address = Base + (i × sizeof(type))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Since addition is commutative (<code>a + b == b + a</code>), <code>*(arr + i)</code> is identical to <code>*(i + arr)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Therefore, in C: <code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i) ≡ *(i + arr) ≡ i[arr]</code>! Writing <code>3[arr]</code> gives 40!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Pointer Increments: <code>*p++</code> vs <code>(*p)++</code> vs <code>*++p</code>:</b><br/><div class='space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm my-2'>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*p++</code>:</b> Reads <code>*p</code> value first, then increments pointer <code>p</code> to point to next integer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>(*p)++</code>:</b> Pointer <code>p</code> does NOT move! Increments the actual integer in memory by 1 (e.g. 10 becomes 11).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*++p</code>:</b> Advances pointer <code>p</code> to next slot first, then dereferences that new slot.</div>• <b>4. The Giant Trap: <code>arr + 1</code> vs <code>&arr + 1</code>:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>arr + 1</code> jumps <b>1 element (4 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1004.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>&arr + 1</code> jumps the <b>entire array of 4 ints (16 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1016!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed into a function (like solve(arr)), it decays into a simple pointer! Inside the function, sizeof(arr) gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NEVER the array size!'}, {'number': '13.2', 'title': 'Short-Circuit Evaluation & Side Effects in C', 'content': '• <b>What is Short-Circuiting?</b> C guarantees left-to-right evaluation for logical operators and stops as soon as truth value is known.<br/>• <b>1. Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 == 0 (False)</code>, the whole result MUST be False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b> Any increments or assignments inside expr2 are completely skipped.<br/>• <b>2. Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 != 0 (True)</code>, the whole result MUST be True.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b><br/><br/>• <b>GATE Code Trace Example:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-amber-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>int a = 1, b = 0;<br/>int res = (a-- || ++b);<br/>printf("%d %d %d", a, b, res);<br/>// Trace: a-- evaluates to 1 (True), then decrements a to 0.<br/>// Because 1 is True, || short-circuits! ++b is NEVER touched!<br/>// Output: 0 0 1</div>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions, watch out for skipped side effects! In (x || y++), if x evaluates to true, y++ is NOT executed, and y remains unchanged!'}, {'number': '13.3', 'title': 'Storage Classes: static, auto, register, extern', 'content': '• <b>1. auto:</b> Default for local variables. Stored on Call Stack. Created when block entered, destroyed when exited.<br/>• <b>2. static:</b> Stored in Data Segment (not stack!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Initialized only ONCE</b> when program loads (defaults to 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Retains its value across multiple recursive calls or function invocations.<br/>• <b>3. register:</b> Request to keep variable in CPU register. Address-of operator <code>&var</code> is ILLEGAL.<br/>• <b>4. extern:</b> References a global variable defined in another file without allocating new storage.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In recursion questions with static int count = 0;, the line static int count = 0; is executed ONLY ONCE on the very first call. On all subsequent recursive calls, it is skipped!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Tracing Pointer Expressions', 'problem': 'Given: int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40}; int *p = a; What does *p++, (*p)++, *++p evaluate to step-by-step?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pointer p starts at a[0] (value 10, address 1000).', 'Step 2: Execute *p++: Reads 10, then pointer p advances to a[1] (address 1004).', 'Step 3: Execute (*p)++: p points to a[1]. Reads 20, then increments the value inside a[1] from 20 to 21. p remains at a[1].', 'Step 4: Execute *++p: Pointer p advances to a[2] (address 1008) first, then reads value 30.', 'Step 5: Array is now {10, 21, 30, 40} and p points to a[2].'], 'answer': 'Final array = {10, 21, 30, 40}; pointer p points to a[2] (30).'}], 'exam_traps': ["In C, sizeof('a') is sizeof(int) = 4 bytes! In C++, sizeof('a') is 1 byte (char).", 'Array boundary overflow in C does NOT throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException! It results in undefined behavior (silent memory corruption).']}, ('PDS', 1): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Linear Data Structures - Stacks, Queues & Linked Lists', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Plate Dispenser & Ticket Counter', 'text': '• Stack (LIFO): The spring-loaded plate stack in a cafeteria. The last plate placed on top is the first one taken by a customer.\n• Queue (FIFO): A movie ticket line. The first person to arrive is the first person served.\n• Circular Queue: Like seats on a Ferris wheel—when the last seat passes, the wheel circles back to seat #1.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '14.1', 'title': 'Stack Infix to Postfix Conversion & Evaluation', 'content': '• <b>Why Postfix (Reverse Polish Notation)?</b> Computers evaluate postfix expressions in 1 linear pass without parentheses or backtracking.<br/>• <b>Postfix Evaluation Algorithm:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan expression from left to right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number): Push onto stack.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If operator: Pop two operands: <code>op2 = pop()</code> (right operand), <code>op1 = pop()</code> (left operand).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Calculate: <code>result = op1 (operator) op2</code>. Push result back onto stack.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Valid Stack Permutations (Catalan Numbers):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total permutations obtainable using push/pop on n distinct elements:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Values: C_1 = 1, C_2 = 2, C_3 = 5, C_4 = 14, C_5 = 42.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation, the FIRST popped element is the RIGHT operand, and the SECOND popped element is the LEFT operand: op1 - op2 (order matters for subtraction and division)!'}, {'number': '14.2', 'title': 'Circular Queue Boundary Conditions', 'content': '• In a linear array queue, dequeuing leaves dead space at the front that cannot be reused without shifting.<br/>• <b>Circular Queue Modulo Operations (Array Size N):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue(x):</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % N; queue[rear] = x;</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue():</b> <code>front = (front + 1) % N; val = queue[front];</code><br/>• <b>Full Condition:</b> <code>(rear + 1) % N == front</code> (leaves 1 empty slot to distinguish from empty!).<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <code>front == rear</code>.<br/>• <b>Current Element Count:</b> <code>(rear - front + N) % N</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a circular queue of array size N that leaves 1 slot empty, the maximum capacity is (N - 1) elements!'}, {'number': '14.3', 'title': "Linked Lists & Floyd's Cycle Detection", 'content': "• <b>Floyd's Tortoise and Hare Cycle Finding:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Slow pointer moves 1 node per step; Fast pointer moves 2 nodes per step.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If a cycle exists, Fast will catch up and meet Slow inside the loop in O(N) time and O(1) space!<br/>• <b>Finding the Starting Node of the Cycle:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Once Slow and Fast meet, keep Fast at the meeting point.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Reset Slow to the <code>head</code> of the list.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Advance both pointers 1 step at a time. The node where they meet is the exact cycle entry point!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Reversing a singly linked list in-place requires O(N) time and strictly O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers: prev, curr, and next.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Evaluating Postfix with Stack', 'problem': 'Evaluate the postfix expression: 12 4 / 3 * 7 2 - +', 'steps': ['Step 1: Push 12, Push 4. Stack: [12, 4].', "Step 2: '/' arrives: op2 = 4, op1 = 12. Compute 12 / 4 = 3. Push 3. Stack: [3].", 'Step 3: Push 3. Stack: [3, 3].', "Step 4: '*' arrives: op2 = 3, op1 = 3. Compute 3 * 3 = 9. Push 9. Stack: [9].", 'Step 5: Push 7, Push 2. Stack: [9, 7, 2].', "Step 6: '-' arrives: op2 = 2, op1 = 7. Compute 7 - 2 = 5. Push 5. Stack: [9, 5].", "Step 7: '+' arrives: op2 = 5, op1 = 9. Compute 9 + 5 = 14. Push 14. Stack: [14]."], 'answer': 'Final evaluated value = 14.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In postfix conversion, operators with higher or EQUAL precedence on the stack must be popped first for left-associative operators!', 'In queue implemented using two stacks, amortized time per operation is O(1), but worst-case single dequeue can take O(N).']}, ('PDS', 2): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Trees, Binary Search Trees & Binary Heaps', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Corporate Hierarchy & Emergency Room Triage', 'text': '• Binary Tree: Like a management chart where every manager has at most two direct reports (left and right vice presidents).\n• Priority Queue (Heap): Like an emergency room triage where doctors treat patients by severity of injury (priority), not arrival order!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '15.1', 'title': 'Binary Tree Mathematical Properties & Traversals', 'content': '• <b>Key Mathematical Theorems for Binary Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Leaf vs Degree-2 Nodes:</b> In any binary tree: <b>n_0 = n_2 + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Number of leaf nodes is ALWAYS 1 more than the number of nodes with 2 children!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Max nodes at height h (root at h=0):</b> <b>2^(h + 1) - 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Strictly Binary Tree with n internal nodes:</b> Has exactly <b>2n edges</b> and <b>n + 1 leaves</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tree Reconstruction from Traversals:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Postorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Postorder:</b> CANNOT uniquely construct a general binary tree!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inorder traversal MUST be given to reconstruct a unique binary tree! Without Inorder, left and right subtrees cannot be separated.'}, {'number': '15.2', 'title': 'Binary Search Trees (BST) & AVL Balance Rotations', 'content': '• <b>BST Inorder Property:</b> Inorder traversal of ANY valid BST ALWAYS yields keys in strictly <b>ASCENDING SORTED ORDER</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of distinct BSTs with n distinct keys:</b> Catalan Number <b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>• <b>AVL Trees (Self-Balancing BST):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Balance Factor = <code>Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree) ∈ {-1, 0, +1}</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Balance Factor becomes ±2, rebalance via 4 rotations:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LL:</b> Single right rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RR:</b> Single left rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LR:</b> Double rotation (Left on child, Right on root).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RL:</b> Double rotation (Right on child, Left on root).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any BST, the node with maximum value has NO right child, and the node with minimum value has NO left child!'}, {'number': '15.3', 'title': 'Binary Heaps & Build-Heap O(n) Complexity', 'content': '• <b>Array Representation (1-based indexing for node at index i):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent = <code>⌊i / 2⌋</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left Child = <code>2i</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right Child = <code>2i + 1</code>.<br/>• <b>Heap Complexities:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Find Min/Max: <b>O(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Insert key: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Extract Min/Max: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Build-Heap (from arbitrary array):</b> <b>O(n) linear time!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Why O(n)? Most nodes are near leaves with tiny heights: ∑ (h / 2^h) converges to 2.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Building a binary heap takes O(n) time, NOT O(n log n)! In a Min-Heap of n elements, the maximum element is always at one of the leaf nodes (indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 to n).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Reconstructing Binary Tree', 'problem': 'Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. What is the Postorder traversal?', 'steps': ['Step 1: First element in Preorder is Root = A.', 'Step 2: Find A in Inorder: Left subtree is {D, B, E}; Right subtree is {F, C}.', 'Step 3: In Preorder, next 3 items {B, D, E} belong to Left subtree ⇒ B is root of Left subtree.', 'Step 4: In Inorder, D is left of B, E is right of B.', 'Step 5: For Right subtree: Preorder has C then F; Inorder has F before C ⇒ C is root, F is left child.', 'Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.'], 'answer': 'Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In a Max-Heap with n elements, finding the minimum element takes O(n) time, because you must scan all leaf nodes!', 'Deleting an arbitrary element from a heap takes O(log n) time provided its index is known; finding it takes O(n).']}, ('ALGO', 0): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis, Master Theorem & Recurrences', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Delivery Speed of Transport Modes', 'text': "• Big-O: An upper bound promise. Like saying 'a flight from Delhi to Mumbai takes at most 3 hours'. It might take 2 hours, but will never exceed 3 hours.\n• Big-Omega (Ω): A lower bound guarantee. 'Walking from Delhi to Mumbai will take at least 5 days'.\n• Big-Theta (Θ): A tight bound. Both upper and lower bounds match."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '16.1', 'title': 'Asymptotic Growth Hierarchy & Limits', 'content': "• <b>Standard Rate of Growth Hierarchy (Memorize for GATE!):</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-yellow-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>c < log(log n) < log n < (log n)² < √n < n < n log n < n² < n³ < 2^n < 3^n < n! < n^n</div>• <b>Testing Growth Rates with Limits:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compute <code>L = lim_{n→∞} [ f(n) / g(n) ]</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = 0: f(n) grows strictly slower: <code>f(n) = o(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = constant (0 < L < ∞): <code>f(n) = Θ(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = ∞: f(n) grows strictly faster: <code>f(n) = ω(g(n))</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Polynomials always beat logarithms: (log n)^1000 = o(n^0.001) for sufficiently large n! Exponentials always beat polynomials: n^1000 = o(1.001^n)!'}, {'number': '16.2', 'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide-and-Conquer Recurrences', 'content': '• For recurrence <b>T(n) = a · T(n / b) + Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b> (where a ≥ 1, b > 1):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compare <b>a</b> with <b>b^k</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Case 1 (a > b^k):</b> Root work dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 2 (a = b^k):</b> Work balanced across levels:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p > -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p = -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log(log n))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 3 (a < b^k):</b> Work at leaf level dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n: a = 2, b = 2, k = 1 ⇒ a = b^k, but p = -1! By Case 2 (p = -1): T(n) = Θ(n log(log n))!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Applying Master Theorem', 'problem': 'Solve T(n) = 8 T(n / 2) + n² log² n.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify: a = 8, b = 2, f(n) = n² log² n ⇒ k = 2, p = 2.', 'Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2² = 4.', 'Step 3: Compare a with b^k: a = 8 > 4 = b^k ⇒ Case 1!', 'Step 4: Exponent log_b a = log_2 8 = 3.', 'Step 5: Therefore, T(n) = Θ(n^3).'], 'answer': 'T(n) = Θ(n³).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Master Theorem cannot be applied if a is not constant (e.g. 2^n T(n/2)) or if b is not constant!', 'Master theorem for subtracting recurrences: T(n) = T(n - 1) + n gives Θ(n²); T(n) = T(n - 1) + 1 gives Θ(n).']}, ('ALGO', 1): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Sorting Algorithms, Lower Bounds & Hash Tables', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Playing Card Hand & Hotel Keycard Racks', 'text': '• Insertion Sort: Like sorting cards in your hand—you pick each new card and slide it into its correct position among already sorted cards.\n• Hash Table: Like a hotel keycard rack. The room number directly maps to the hook without searching through all keys.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '17.1', 'title': 'Master Summary Table of Sorting Algorithms', 'content': "• <b>Complete Comparison Table for GATE CSE:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Algorithm      Best Time     Avg Time      Worst Time    Space     Stable?   Method<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Insertion Sort O(n)          O(n²)         O(n²)         O(1)      YES       Incremental<br/>Merge Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n)      YES       Divide & Conquer<br/>Heap Sort      O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(1)      NO        Selection/Heap<br/>Quick Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n²)         O(log n)  NO        Partitioning<br/>Counting Sort  O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(k)      YES       Non-comparison</div>• <b>Stability Definition:</b> Preserves the relative order of records with equal keys.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The theoretical lower bound for any comparison-based sorting algorithm in the worst case is Ω(n log n)! Non-comparison sorts (Counting, Radix) achieve O(n) by using array indexing instead of comparisons.'}, {'number': '17.2', 'title': 'Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing', 'content': "• <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Each slot holds a linked list. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/>• <b>Open Addressing (All keys stored in table, α ≤ 1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b> (long blocks of occupied slots).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) % m</code>. Eliminates primary clustering; suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i · h2(k)) % m</code>. Eliminates both clusterings (h2(k) must be relatively prime to m).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In open addressing, expected number of probes for unsuccessful search = 1 / (1 - α); for successful search = (1 / α) ln(1 / (1 - α))!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Double Hashing Probe Sequence', 'problem': 'Insert key 75 into a table of size 11 using h1(k) = k % 11 and h2(k) = 1 + (k % 10). If slot h1(75) is occupied, find the next probe slot.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Primary hash h1(75) = 75 % 11 = 9.', 'Step 2: Slot 9 is occupied ⇒ collision!', 'Step 3: Secondary hash h2(75) = 1 + (75 % 10) = 1 + 5 = 6.', 'Step 4: Next probe for i = 1: (h1(k) + 1 × h2(k)) % 11 = (9 + 6) % 11 = 15 % 11 = 4.'], 'answer': 'Next probe slot is index 4.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In QuickSort, if the array is ALREADY SORTED and the pivot is chosen as first or last element, QuickSort degrades to its WORST-CASE O(n²) time!', 'In hashing with open addressing, deleted slots must be marked with a special DELETED marker; setting to NULL breaks probe chains.']}, ('ALGO', 2): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Greedy Algorithms, Dynamic Programming & Divide-and-Conquer', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Making Change & Remembering Past Math Results', 'text': "• Greedy: Like a cashier giving change with the fewest coins by picking the largest coin possible first (25c, then 10c, etc.).\n• Dynamic Programming: 'What is 1 + 1 + 1 + 1? 4! Now add another + 1? 5!' You didn't re-count 1 to 4 because you REMEMBERED the previous result (Memoization)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '18.1', 'title': 'Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming', 'content': "• <b>Greedy Method:</b> Makes locally optimal choice at each step without backtracking.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Huffman Coding, Kruskal's, Prim's, Dijkstra.<br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b> Explores all possibilities by breaking into subproblems, but solves each subproblem only once.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Requires <b>Optimal Substructure</b> AND <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved by Greedy (sort by value/weight in O(n log n)). 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved by Greedy; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n × W) pseudo-polynomial time!'}, {'number': '18.2', 'title': 'Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence', 'content': '• For strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] == Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i - 1, j - 1]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] ≠ Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i - 1, j], LCS[i, j - 1])</code>.<br/>• <b>Time & Space:</b> O(m × n) time; space can be optimized to O(min(m, n)) using only two rows.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Matrix Chain Multiplication with n matrices, number of different parenthesizations is given by Catalan Number C_{n-1}!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Huffman Code Bit Length', 'problem': 'Frequencies: A: 45, B: 13, C: 12, D: 16, E: 9, F: 5. Construct Huffman tree and find total bits required.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Merge smallest two: F(5) + E(9) = 14.', 'Step 2: Merge next two smallest: C(12) + B(13) = 25.', 'Step 3: Merge 14 and D(16) = 30.', 'Step 4: Merge 25 and 30 = 55.', 'Step 5: Merge A(45) and 55 = 100.', 'Step 6: Code lengths: A = 1 bit; B, C, D = 3 bits; E, F = 4 bits.', 'Step 7: Total bits = (45×1) + (13×3) + (12×3) + (16×3) + (9×4) + (5×4) = 45 + 39 + 36 + 48 + 36 + 20 = 224 bits.'], 'answer': 'Total encoded length = 224 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Huffman code is a prefix code: No codeword is a prefix of another!', 'Huffman tree is always a strictly full binary tree with 2n - 1 total nodes for n characters.']}, ('ALGO', 3): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Graph Traversals, MST & Shortest Paths', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: GPS Navigation & Fiber-Optic City Network', 'text': "• Dijkstra's Algorithm: How Google Maps finds the fastest driving route to your destination.\n• Minimum Spanning Tree: Laying down high-speed fiber cables between cities with the minimum total cost while ensuring every city is connected."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '19.1', 'title': 'BFS vs DFS Comparison & Applications', 'content': "• <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects bipartiteness (2-coloring).<br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b> Uses a <b>Stack / Recursion</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects cycles in directed graphs (via <b>Back Edges</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computes <b>Topological Sort</b> of a DAG (reverse of finish times).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's / Tarjan's algorithm).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of a directed graph, a cycle exists IF AND ONLY IF there is at least one BACK EDGE! Forward and Cross edges do NOT indicate a cycle.'}, {'number': '19.2', 'title': 'Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall', 'content': "• <b>Dijkstra's Algorithm:</b> Single-Source Shortest Path for <b>non-negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <b>O((V + E) log V)</b> using min-heap. FAILS for negative edge weights!<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm:</b> Works with <b>negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relaxes all edges (V - 1) times. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects negative-weight cycles (if distance decreases on V-th relaxation).<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path in <b>O(V³)</b> time using dynamic programming.<br/>• <b>Kruskal vs Prim (MST):</b> Kruskal uses edges (O(E log E) with DSU); Prim grows from a vertex (O(E + V log V)).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights in a graph are distinct, the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is 100% UNIQUE! Shortest path tree is NOT necessarily unique.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Kruskal's MST Edge Selection", 'problem': 'Given edges sorted by weight: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (A-C, 2), (C-D, 3), (B-D, 4). Which edges are included in the MST?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pick (A-B, 1) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 2: Pick (B-C, 2) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 3: Consider (A-C, 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT.', 'Step 4: Pick (C-D, 3) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 5: We have 4 vertices and 3 edges (V - 1 = 3). MST is complete!'], 'answer': 'MST edges: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (C-D, 3). Total weight = 6.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Dijkstra's algorithm can enter an infinite loop or give wrong answers if negative edge weights are present!", 'Topological sort exists IF AND ONLY IF the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).']}}
